# ADR-002: Adopt Domain-Driven Design (DDD)

## Status

Accepted

---

## Context

BioResearch AI models a complex biomedical research workflow involving
research questions, scientific publications, evidence synthesis, reports,
citations, and persistent workspaces.

As the platform evolves to support additional literature sources,
biological databases, AI agents, and scientific workflows, the business
logic will become increasingly sophisticated.

A simple data-centric design would couple business rules to infrastructure
and make the domain difficult to evolve and maintain.

---

## Decision

The project will adopt Domain-Driven Design (DDD) to organize the core
business logic around domain concepts rather than technical concerns.

The Domain layer will define:

- Entities
- Value Objects
- Domain Interfaces
- Domain Services (when necessary)

Application services will orchestrate use cases while Infrastructure
provides concrete implementations of external dependencies.

The Domain must remain independent of frameworks, databases, APIs,
and AI providers.

---

## Alternatives Considered

### Anemic Domain Model

Simple to implement but places business logic in services rather than
the domain model.

### Transaction Script

Suitable for CRUD applications but becomes difficult to maintain as
business complexity grows.

### Domain-Driven Design (DDD)

Encapsulates business rules within domain models, improves maintainability,
and aligns naturally with complex scientific workflows.

Chosen.

---

## Consequences

Advantages

- Clear representation of biomedical research concepts
- Strong separation between business logic and infrastructure
- Easier testing of domain behavior
- Improved extensibility as new workflows are introduced
- Better alignment between code and scientific processes

Disadvantages

- Additional design effort
- More abstractions than simpler architectures
- Requires familiarity with DDD principles

Future considerations

As BioResearch AI evolves toward multi-agent scientific reasoning,
knowledge graph construction, and integration with additional biomedical
databases, the domain model can grow without requiring significant changes
to application or infrastructure layers.